WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.280
DAN THAGARD: Hey,
good afternoon.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.710
My name's Dan Thagard.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.630
I'm the director of site
reliability and core

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.640
engineering at Magic Leap.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.350
My team's mission
at Magic Leap is

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.900
to provide technology
solutions and services

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.290
to the rest of the organization,
to enable people to do

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.233
and perform their best work.

00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:25.650
We're a globally
distributed team.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.290
We provide 24/7, 365
support to our customers.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.910
And one of the challenges that
we face as being a global team

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:33.810
is--

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.667
well, apart from
daylight savings,

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:37.500
and realizing that New
Zealand actually goes

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.980
the opposite direction than
the US when the time changes--

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.140
is to kind of maintain
a sense of team

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.750
and maintain a sense of
community and team building.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.560
So last year, we were
casting around for ideas

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.703
for how can we do a
team building exercise.

00:00:52.703 --> 00:00:54.120
And we landed on
the idea of, hey,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.280
why don't we build an
app for Magic Leap One?

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.790
Now, I've got to preface this
by saying that none of us

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.450
are 3D application developers.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.650
We're not modelers, or graphics
artists, or game designers

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.940
whatsoever.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.440
But we are SREs, and we
know how to build and run

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.425
infrastructure, and run
it reliably at scale.

00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:14.370
So we said, hey, wouldn't
it be really cool

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.590
if we can take the
infrastructure that we

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.770
do and work with day to day, and
be able to visualize it using

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.950
spatial computing, to be able
to actually see all the nodes,

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.230
and show the dependencies
and everything that we're

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.147
working on.

00:01:26.147 --> 00:01:27.480
So that's what we decided to do.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.438
That was our challenge.

00:01:28.438 --> 00:01:33.480
We wanted to, in 24 hours, be
able to build and visualize,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.670
using Terraform,
our infrastructure

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.650
and spatial computing.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.010
And that's what we did.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:43.100
So 24 hours later-- well,
about 30 hours, actually.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.020
But in a day, we were able to--

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.050
with no help, no outside
help, no documentation--

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.740
just using what
our customers see,

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:54.000
we were able to build on-device,
having no previous Unity

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.550
experience and no previous
on [INAUDIBLE] or anything

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:57.690
like that.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:02.310
And it was a really empowering
and joyful experience.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.070
I can't describe
how happy that was

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:11.640
the first time I saw this sea
of Leapers appear on stage in 3D

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.210
in front of me.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:16.630
It was kind of like my first
"Hello, world" all over again.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:20.940
One of the challenges that
we faced with this was,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.770
not having any
outside help, we had

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:27.330
to kind of dig within the
team, and leverage each other,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.900
and really work
together as a team.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.983
So I'm going to turn it
over to Anthony right now.

00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:36.442
He's going to talk through some
of the technical challenges

00:02:36.442 --> 00:02:38.010
that we faced as
a team as we were

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.650
trying to build
out infrastructure

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.550
using mixed reality.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.030
ANTHONY LUCILLO: Thanks, Dan.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.280
My name is Anthony Lucillo.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.460
I'm a site reliability
engineer at Magic Leap,

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.220
and the technical
lead of Nebula.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.862
We built this experience
using Unity and C#.

00:03:01.862 --> 00:03:03.320
For the multiplayer
element, we are

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:08.900
using Photon, which handles the
user and room management, as

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.180
well as synchronization of
object data like position,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.130
rotation, scale,
even custom metadata

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.890
that we're able to send.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:22.160
Using Terraform and
a CI/CD pipeline,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.630
we deployed the necessary back
end components, Google Cloud

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:30.350
Functions and Google
Cloud Storage.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:33.380
This allowed us to
pull Terraform state

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:40.820
data, which we then parse and
visualize in mixed reality.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.550
There is so much more that
you can do than simply

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.610
reading a Terraform state file.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:51.740
Imagine, you could make
changes in real time

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:57.650
with Terraform in mixed reality,
query near-live metadata

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:01.760
to facilitate tasks like
architecture reviews, cost

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.870
analysis, and system
health monitoring.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.180
You can even customize
your war rooms

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.550
with dashboards,
comms channels, and

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.310
whatever other
useful information

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:15.350
you could fill the room with.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.320
And because this
is collaborative,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.340
you would all see the exact
same thing in that war room.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.970
In my opinion, the
most important part

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.660
of this experience
is making sure

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.575
that everyone sees the same
thing in the same place.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:40.270
Which brings us to the most
complex and interesting problem

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.020
that we faced--

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:50.040
how do you make sure that every
user in the same experience

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.250
sees the same thing?

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.150
Each device is independent.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.620
Therefore, how can multiple
users in the same experience

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:06.780
know everyone else's
position and orientation?

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.990
Where is the origin?

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.950
There are a few solutions
to this problem.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:18.950
Our experience solves
this using image tracking.

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.600
The Magic Leap image
tracking system

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.630
uses the camera to detect
two-dimensional planar

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.720
images from a
custom-defined target set,

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.340
and continuously tracks
and updates its location

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:38.950
and orientation as users move
around in the real world.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:42.430
This image, therefore,
becomes a point of reference,

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:49.150
a way for everyone to agree on
an orientation and an origin

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:49.780
for the room.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.410
Now that we're through
the technical details,

00:05:55.410 --> 00:06:00.436
let's watch a quick
video of Nebula.

00:06:00.436 --> 00:06:07.540
[AUDIO TRADEMARK PLAYING]

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.287
And here, we're in
the-- oh, it's over.

00:06:09.287 --> 00:06:09.787
OK.

00:06:16.493 --> 00:06:19.860
OK, we're choosing a room.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.380
We've got a couple Terraform
architectures to choose from.

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:29.090
You can drag it out
and into the room

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:30.340
and manipulate their position.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.050
The red indicators
you see are what

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:41.210
other users in the same
experience are looking at.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.190
And as you can see,
other users are also

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:53.017
able to move objects
at the same time,

00:06:53.017 --> 00:06:55.100
and you're able to even
try to steal it from them.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:12.600
And you can also move up
to it and interact with it,

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.760
because it's out there
in the real world.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:18.210
Thank you.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:18.780
SPEAKER 1: Great, thanks.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.197
Big round of applause, please,
for the team at Magic Leap.

00:07:21.197 --> 00:07:24.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

